5 until his death in 453.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attimabbe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attimabbe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attimabbe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attimabbe was a Jain noblewoman of the Western Chalukya court in the late tenth and early eleventh centuries, the widow of the general Nagadeva.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attock"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attock.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attock</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Attock is a town in Punjab, Pakistan, on the Indus near its junction with the Kabul River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/attrition"><span class="enc-name">Attrition</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Attrition refers to a form of warfare in which victory depends on wearing down the enemy's manpower and material resources through continuous losses.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/attus-clausus"><span class="enc-name">Attus Clausus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attus Clausus was a Sabine leader who, according to Roman tradition, moved to Rome around 504 BCE with a large band of followers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/atuatuca"><span class="enc-name">Atuatuca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Atuatuca was a stronghold in the territory of the Eburones in northern Gaul, described by Julius Caesar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/au-clair-de-la-lune"><span class="enc-name">Au clair de la lune</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">&quot;Au clair de la lune&quot; (&quot;By the Light of the Moon&quot;) is a