ut 224 km long.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/audiencia"><span class="enc-name">Audiencia</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">An audiencia was a high court of justice in the Spanish Empire, which in the Americas and the Philippines also exercised administrative and political functions.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/audoleon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/audoleon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Audoleon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Audoleon was king of Paeonia from about 315 to 285 BCE, son of King Patraos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aughnanure-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aughnanure-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aughnanure Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Aughnanure Castle is a six-storey tower house near Oughterard, County Galway, Ireland, built by the O'Flaherty lords of Iar Connacht in the 15th or 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augsburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augsburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augsburg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augsburg is a city in Bavaria, southern Germany, and was a free imperial city of the Holy Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augsburg-confession.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augsburg Confession</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Augsburg Confession is the founding statement of Lutheran belief, drafted by Philip Melanchthon and presented to Emperor Charles V at the Diet of Augsburg in 1530.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-hermann-francke"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-hermann-francke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August Hermann Francke</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">August Hermann Francke (1663–1727) was a German Lutheran clergyman, theologian and educator, a leader of Pietism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Revolution · 1945</span><span class="enc-snip">The August Revolution was the seizure of power across Vietnam by the Viet Minh in August 1945, after Japan's surrender at the end of the Second World War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-von-gneisenau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-von-gneisenau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August von Gneisenau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">August Neidhardt von Gneisenau was a Prussian field marshal and military reformer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-von-kotzebue"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-von-kotzebue.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August von Kotzebue</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">August von Kotzebue was a German playwright and writer, one of the most performed dramatists of his time, who also served the Russian government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusta-raurica"><span class="enc-name">Augusta Raurica</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusta Raurica is a Roman town site at Augst near Basel, Switzerland, on the Rhine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusta-vindelicorum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augusta-vindelicorum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augusta Vindelicorum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusta Vindelicorum was the Roman town that became Augsburg, which grew from a military base set up after the Roman conquest of the Alpine region in 15 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusta-sicily"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augusta-sicily.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augusta, Sicily</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusta is a port town on the east coast of Sicily, between Catania and Syracuse, founded by the emperor Frederick II in 1232.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustal-prefect"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augustal-prefect.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augustal prefect</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Augustal prefect (praefectus augustalis) was the title of the governor of Egypt in the late Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auguste-comte"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auguste-comte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auguste Comte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Auguste Comte was a French philosopher of the nineteenth century and the founder of positivism, which emphasised science, order and progress.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auguste-de-marmont.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auguste de Marmont</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Auguste de Marmont was a French general and Marshal of the Empire under Napoleon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auguste-pavie"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auguste-pavie.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auguste Pavie</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Auguste Pavie was a French explorer and diplomat who mapped much of Laos and Cambodia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustin-calmet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augustin-calmet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augustin Calmet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Augustin Calmet was a French Benedictine monk and scholar from Lorraine, abbot of Senones from 1728.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustinas-voldemaras"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augustinas-voldemaras.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augustinas Voldemaras</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Augustinas Voldemaras was a Lithuanian historian and politician who became the first prime minister of independent Lithuania in November 1918.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-canterbury"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augustine-of-canterbury.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augustine of Canterbury</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Augustine of Canterbury was a monk from Rome sent by Pope Gregory I to convert the Anglo-Saxons.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augustine-of-hippo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augustine of Hippo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Augustine of Hippo was a Christian theologian and bishop from Roman North Africa, born at Thagaste in Numidia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusto-b-leguia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augusto-b-leguia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augusto B. Leguía</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusto B. Leguía was a Peruvian businessman and politician who was president of Peru from 1908 to 1912 and again from 1919 to 1930.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusto-cesar-sandino"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augusto-cesar-sandino.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augusto César Sandino</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusto César Sandino was a Nicaraguan revolutionary who led a guerrilla war against the United States Marines occupying Nicaragua from 1927 to 1933, after rejecting…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augusto-pinochet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augusto Pinochet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusto Pinochet was a Chilean general who led the military coup of 11 September 1973 that overthrew Salvador Allende.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustobriga-lusitania"><span class="enc-name">Augustobriga (Lusitania)</span><span clas